findings.description:
This diff deletes `pruneStaleFiles` from `worktreePropagation.ts` — the only pruner that contained deletions to the worktree. It did so explicitly: `escapesLexically(relPath)` rejected absolute paths, drive letters, and `..` segments, and a realpath check (`isWithin`) guarded against symlinked install directories, with a doc comment stating "manifest paths come from a user-editable file" and a full test suite (`worktreePrune.test.ts`, also deleted) covering `../outside.md` entries. With the copy-and-prune fallback gone, `InstallManifest.finish()` is now the *only* pruning path — including for worktrees, which previously got the containment-checked pruner. In `finish()`, stale candidates are `pathField(oldLines)` joined straight into the root with no lexical or realpath check:

```ts
const file = path.join(this.targetRoot, rel);
if (rel === '' || !isFile(file)) continue;
if (oldLines.includes(`${cksumField(fs.readFileSync(file))} ${rel}`)) {
  fs.rmSync(file);
  this.removeEmptyInstallDirs(rel);
```

A hand-edited `.context-forge/<target>.manifest` line such as `<crc> <size> ../../sibling/file` (the CRC is trivially computable from the published `cksum`) resolves outside the checkout and is deleted, and `removeEmptyInstallDirs` then rmdirs upward from the joined path. Exploitability is low — the manifest is in the victim's own checkout and the CRC must match — but this was a trust boundary the codebase itself called out, defended, and tested, and the diff removes the defense without a comment recording the decision. The root checkout had this exposure before the diff (it always used `finish()`); worktrees are newly exposed. Add a `..`/absolute filter on `rel` (and ideally the realpath check) before `fs.rmSync`, or document in `manifest.ts` that manifest contents are now trusted input and why.
